-- Schema evolution with a migration clause and the schema lock.
--
-- A relation's stored schema can change over time. The source holds ONLY the
-- current schema; history lives in `<name>.schema.lock` (append-only, owned by
-- `knot lock`, never written by `knot build`). To change a schema:
--
--   1. Edit the schema and add a pending migration clause: a bare lambda
--      `\old -> <new row>` directly under the `Rel` declaration (the old schema
--      is derived from the lock's last schema; the target is this source's
--      declared type). The lambda is a hand-written upgrade applied to each
--      stored row.
--   2. `knot build` warns the migration is uncommitted, and running the binary
--      applies it on a FORK of the database — the main DB is never touched.
--   3. `knot lock` commits the migration into the lock and strips the block
--      from this source. The next run fast-forwards the main DB.
--
-- NOTE: relation columns must use ADT flags, not the primitive `Bool` — a
-- `Bool` column panics in the storage layer (pre-existing runtime issue).
with {

Active  Yes {}  No {}

-- The current schema: people have a name and an `active` flag. The previous
-- version (name only) and the migration that upgraded it live in
-- `examples/migrate.schema.lock`, committed by an earlier `knot lock`.
Person  {name Text  active Active}

Rel Person  *people

}
(do
  full *people = [{name "Alice"  active (Active.Yes {})}  {name "Bob"  active (Active.No {})}]
  rows <- *people
  base.println (base.show rows))
